1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"partial\",\"rationale\":\"The answer gets both central conclusions right. It says APs are generally not paid by the sponsor for having AP status. It says creation and redemption fees are charged to APs and go to the fund, not to the AP. It also states that creation and redemption are voluntary, with no ongoing obligation. It correctly treats sponsor liquidity-provider or incentive programs as separate arrangements, which matches the grader note that market-maker/LMM contracts fall outside AP-status compensation. However, it leaves out two key points. First, it never says sponsors or advisers may waive or pay the transaction fee, which subsidises the AP's cost. In particular, it omits the vouched point that sponsors often waive fees on custom redemptions, which the grader notes say to preserve. Second, it does not distinguish dealer APs from agency APs, which can charge clients for processing creations and redemptions. The claim that market makers have no general regulatory duty to quote also overstates things, since exchange-registered market makers do have quoting obligations, though this is a minor point. Because the waiver/absorption point is directly relevant to whether the sponsor pays APs and was not addressed, the grade is partial rather than correct.\"}","ttft_ms":5227,"type":"result","duration_ms":8581,"uuid":"bc4f4b2c-8aa4-4898-9db5-86c1e6df4285","ttft_stream_ms":525,"time_to_request_ms":57,"first_content_frame_ms":525,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
